\par\par \b \uc2  IMF approves extra $280 million loans for Romania. \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 1995\par 21:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, Dec 21 (Reuter) - The International Monetary Fund said on Thursday that it approved an extra $280 million in loans for Romania to help the country rein in rising consumer prices and a ballooning trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  The additional money will come in the form of an extension of an existing IMF "standby" credit for Romania through April 1997, it said. \par \par \uc2  Romania's monthly inflation rate accelerated in November to its highest level this year, 4.1 percent, from 3.5 percent in October. Since last December, prices have risen 23 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rise has been fuelled by a steep drop in Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the leu, brought on by a widening of the trade deficit. \b \highlight22\uc2 Romania\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the Bucharest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday to support the leu. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said its loans will be used to back up an economic programme aimed at reducing inflation to less than 20 percent in 1996, cutting the trade deficit and significantly building up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drcl00lz4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
